      },
      {
        "name": "accent",
        "value": {
          "light": "#e3f047",
          "dark": "#e3f047"
        },
        "scoped": "oklch(var(--cipher-accent-lc, 0.917 0.183) {hue})",
        "usage": "The one accent. Outside every hue scope it is Infisical yellow #e3f047 in both themes (sign-in, and any page that sets no hue); inside a hue scope it is that hue's own accent, which hueScope places at the lightest point where the hue holds the brand chroma, so a KMS create button is KMS green and the brand hue still lands on #e3f047. The fill of the one primary create action on a page (Add new, Create key, New connection), approval and grant buttons, counts (in the sidebar and the bulk action bar), the on switch track in dark, the slider range in dark and search highlights; the current page in the sidebar stays neutral. On light grounds a yellow fill is nearly as light as the ground and carries no edge: it is a highlight, and what it says is its on-accent text, so the label, not the fill, identifies the control or the number. Never let a bare fill with no text carry meaning (a dot, a bar); use accent-fg for those. A fill, never text on light grounds. For accent-colored text and icons (the banner), use accent-fg."
      },
      {
        "name": "accent-2",
        "value": {
          "light": "#d3df29",
          "dark": "#d1de23"
        },
        "scoped": "oklch(var(--cipher-accent-2-lc, 0.867 0.183) {hue})",
        "usage": "Hover and pressed fill of accent buttons. A step darker than accent on the same hue (0.05 lower oklch lightness, chroma kept where the gamut allows); on-accent on it passes AA on every hue."
      },
      {
        "name": "on-accent",
        "value": {
          "light": "#010101",
          "dark": "#010101"
        },
        "scoped": "var(--cipher-accent-on, #010101)",
        "usage": "Text and icons on an accent fill. Near-black outside every hue scope, the same in both themes. Inside a scope hueScope picks near-black or white, whichever keeps the higher contrast on the worst of the accent, accent-2 and the lightest stop of the button material, so a dark accent (a deep blue) gets white text. Every product hue picks near-black."
      },
      {
